---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import ChapterLabel from '../../components/global/ChapterLabel.astro'

const title = 'Claude Code Memory Systems: Complete Guide 2026'
const description = 'Claude Code memory in 2026: memory types as four axes, what stays local vs what the team shares, whether injected memory is actually used, and the native stack, cross-session tools (claude-mem, agentmemory, memorix, ICM) and team options, checked at source.'

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "Claude Code Memory Systems: Complete Reference 2026",
    "description": "Three-track memory model: native stack, cross-session tools, team and multi-agent sharing. Tool comparison, decision flowchart, benchmarks.",
    "url": "https://cc.bruniaux.com/memory-systems/",
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://cc.bruniaux.com/memory-systems/",
    "author": { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.florian.bruniaux.com/#person", "name": "Florian BRUNIAUX", "url": "https://github.com/FlorianBruniaux", "jobTitle": "Fractional Chief AI Officer" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "@id": "https://cc.bruniaux.com/#organization", "name": "Claude Code Ultimate Guide", "url": "https://cc.bruniaux.com" },
    "datePublished": "2026-05-23",
    "dateModified": "2026-11-03",
    "inLanguage": "en",
    "keywords": "Claude Code memory, CLAUDE.md, Auto Memory, Auto Dream, claude-mem, agentmemory, memorix, basic-memory, ICM, Kairn, doobidoo, Mem0, cross-session memory, agent memory, episodic memory, procedural memory, shared memory, memory pipeline"
  }
]

const nativeTools = [
  {
    name: "CLAUDE.md",
    badge: "Core",
    badgeColor: "accent",
    desc: "Permanent instructions injected at three levels: global (~/.claude/CLAUDE.md), project (./CLAUDE.md), and local (.claude/CLAUDE.md). The primary persistence layer for all Claude Code users.",
    highlights: [
      "Global → Project → Local merge order (additive)",
      "@path/to/file.md imports for modularity",
      "200-line limit per file for reliable attention",
      "ETH Zürich finding: developer-written +4% adherence vs LLM-generated -3%",
    ],
    anchor: "21-claudemd-three-levels-of-memory",
  },
  {
    name: "Auto Memory",
    badge: "v2.1.59+",
    badgeColor: "info",
    desc: "Claude writes its own MEMORY.md across sessions without manual calls. Tied to the repository, but machine-local: all worktrees of one repository share one memory directory, and nothing syncs across machines or cloud environments.",
    highlights: [
      "200-line / 25 KB index limit",
      "Shared by every worktree of the same repository",
      "Excluded from transcript cleanup: stays until edited or deleted",
      "Replaces manual 'remember X' patterns",
    ],
    anchor: "22-auto-memory-v2159",
  },
  {
    name: "Auto Dream",
    badge: "Community",
    badgeColor: "warning",
    desc: "Background sub-agent that consolidates memory after ≥5 sessions AND ≥24h. Community-discovered, not officially documented. Runs a 4-phase cycle: Orient / Gather / Consolidate / Prune.",
    highlights: [
      "Triggers: ≥5 sessions + ≥24h gap (both required)",
      "4-phase: Orient → Gather → Consolidate → Prune",
      "Merges duplicates, removes stale entries",
      "Not in official docs or the changelog (checked Nov 3, 2026)",
    ],
    anchor: "23-auto-dream-memory-consolidation",
  },
  {
    name: "Agent Memory",
    badge: "v2.1.33+",
    badgeColor: "info",
    desc: "Frontmatter field `memory: user|project|local` in agent definition files. Injects up to 200 lines of the corresponding MEMORY.md at agent startup.",
    highlights: [
      "Three scopes: user (global), project, local",
      "Injected before any user message",
      "Project scope lives in .claude/agent-memory/ and can be committed",
      "Inert when auto memory is disabled",
    ],
    anchor: "24-agent-memory-frontmatter",
  },
]

const crossSessionTools = [
  {
    name: "claude-mem",
    stars: "111K",
    license: "Apache-2.0",
    approach: "9 hook events + LLM observer; injects the newest 50 observations at session start",
    storage: "SQLite + Chroma (on by default)",
    cost: "Your Claude subscription by default",
    recall: "Smart 3-step search",
    install: "/plugin install claude-mem",
    anchor: "31-claude-mem",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "agentmemory",
    stars: "30K",
    license: "Apache-2.0",
    approach: "12 hooks capture; injection opt-in; BM25 + Vector + Graph RRF",
    storage: "iii file store or Redis",
    cost: "Zero LLM by default",
    recall: "95.2% R@5 LongMemEval-S (self-reported)",
    install: "npm i -g @agentmemory/agentmemory",
    anchor: "32-agentmemory",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "memorix",
    stars: "896",
    license: "Apache-2.0",
    approach: "One memory pool across ~18 coding agents, MCP + optional hooks",
    storage: "SQLite, scoped to a project by Git identity",
    cost: "Free (local)",
    recall: "Consolidation and retention from a dashboard",
    install: "memorix setup --agent <name>",
    anchor: "38-other-notable-tools",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "basic-memory",
    stars: "4.5K",
    license: "AGPL-3.0",
    approach: "Plain Markdown you can edit, Claude Code plugin hooks",
    storage: "Markdown + SQLite index",
    cost: "Free locally; paid cloud for teams",
    recall: "Hybrid search",
    install: null,
    anchor: "38-other-notable-tools",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "ICM",
    stars: null,
    license: "Source-available",
    approach: "Episodic decay + permanent graph",
    storage: "SQLite + semantic index",
    cost: "Free (local)",
    recall: "Dual: Memories + Memoirs",
    install: "brew install icm",
    anchor: "33-icm-infinite-context-memory",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "Kairn",
    stars: null,
    license: "MIT",
    approach: "Biological decay model",
    storage: "Local graph DB",
    cost: "Free (local)",
    recall: "~200d solutions, ~50d workarounds",
    install: "pip install kairn",
    anchor: "34-kairn",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "doobidoo",
    stars: "2.1K",
    license: "Apache-2.0",
    approach: "MCP + REST server, Codex hooks",
    storage: "SQLite-vec (default)",
    cost: "Free (local)",
    recall: "Vector similarity",
    install: "pip install mcp-memory-service",
    anchor: "35-doobidoo-mcp-memory-service",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "OpenMemory MCP",
    stars: null,
    license: "Apache 2.0",
    approach: "MCP server (Mem0 AI)",
    storage: "Local or cloud",
    cost: "Free tier available",
    recall: "Semantic search",
    install: "claude mcp add mem0",
    anchor: "36-openmemory-mcp",
    highlight: false,
  },
]

const teamOptions = [
  {
    name: "The Trinity",
    badge: "Zero infra",
    badgeColor: "accent",
    desc: "Commit CLAUDE.md + .mcp.json + /skills to the repo. Any developer who clones gets the full memory state immediately: no server, no account, no sync step.",
    when: "Team with shared conventions, no infra budget",
    anchor: "41-the-trinity-claudemd--mcpjson--skills",
  },
  {
    name: "Mem0 Cloud MCP",
    badge: "Free tier",
    badgeColor: "info",
    desc: "Shared memory pool accessible to all team members via MCP server. One-command install, optional self-host. Works across sessions and users.",
    when: "Small team needing shared context without self-hosting",
    anchor: "43-mem0-cloud-mcp",
  },
  {
    name: "Zep / Graphiti",
    badge: "Advanced",
    badgeColor: "warning",
    desc: "Temporal knowledge graph with bitemporal edge windows: tracks not just what was true, but when it was true and when it was learned.",
    when: "Complex projects where history and causality matter",
    anchor: "44-zep--graphiti",
  },
]

const decisionRules = [
  {
    condition: "Solo developer, CLAUDE.md covers the context",
    answer: "Native Stack: enable Auto Memory, costs nothing, zero setup",
    track: "native",
  },
  {
    condition: "Re-explaining context every session wastes 10+ minutes",
    answer: "claude-mem or agentmemory: both auto-capture without explicit calls",
    track: "cross",
  },
  {
    condition: "Need causal chains: 'this bug caused that regression'",
    answer: "Kairn (biological decay) or ICM Memoirs (typed relation graph)",
    track: "cross",
  },
  {
    condition: "Team memory, zero infra budget",
    answer: "The Trinity: CLAUDE.md + .mcp.json + skills committed to repo",
    track: "team",
  },
  {
    condition: "Team memory, okay with a lightweight cloud service",
    answer: "Mem0 Cloud MCP: free tier, one-command install",
    track: "team",
  },
  {
    condition: "Several coding agents on one machine should share what they learn",
    answer: "memorix: one project pool across Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and others",
    track: "cross",
  },
  {
    condition: "Multi-agent pipeline, concurrent writes",
    answer: "Lease + signal pattern on top of any persistent store",
    track: "team",
  },
  {
    condition: "Before keeping any memory layer",
    answer: "Plant a canary, then run your own tasks with memory on and off",
    track: "native",
  },
]

const memoryAxes = [
  { axis: "Content", values: "Working state, episodes, facts, procedures, reference documents", example: "MEMORY.md holds facts; a skill holds a procedure" },
  { axis: "Form", values: "Context tokens, files, database or graph, model weights", example: "CLAUDE.md is a file that becomes context tokens at load" },
  { axis: "Scope", values: "Turn, session, machine, repository, team service, organization, agent fleet", example: "Auto memory is tied to a repository yet stays on one machine" },
  { axis: "Lifecycle", values: "Write, retrieve, consolidate, supersede, forget", example: "Compaction rewrites working state; Auto Dream consolidates" },
]

const memoryScopes = [
  { mechanism: "Claude Code auto memory", scope: "One machine, every worktree of the repository", shared: "Not across machines or cloud", lifecycle: "Stays until edited or deleted" },
  { mechanism: "Claude Code subagent memory:", scope: "user, project or local", shared: "project scope can be committed", lifecycle: "Inert when auto memory is off" },
  { mechanism: "Managed CLAUDE.md", scope: "Organization-managed machines", shared: "Deployed by administrators", lifecycle: "Guidance, not enforcement" },
  { mechanism: "Codex memories", scope: "Local store under ~/.codex", shared: "Separate from ChatGPT memory", lifecycle: "Off by default, updated in the background" },
  { mechanism: "Gemini CLI Auto Memory", scope: "Local transcripts", shared: "Drafts patches and SKILL.md for review", lifecycle: "Experimental, never applied without approval" },
  { mechanism: "GitHub Copilot Memory", scope: "Repository", shared: "Facts shared with collaborators, cited to code", lifecycle: "Expire after 90 days unless upvoted" },
  { mechanism: "Kiro memory", scope: "Local user account", shared: "IDE and CLI on one machine", lifecycle: "Not documented" },
]

const pipelineStages = ["captured", "stored", "indexed", "retrievable", "injected", "effective"]
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow">Claude Code</div>
      <h1>Memory systems</h1>
      <p class="hero-desc">Claude Code runs on three distinct memory tracks, not one. Knowing which fits your situation determines whether sessions start from scratch or pick up exactly where you left off. Knowing whether the memory is actually used decides whether it is worth keeping.</p>
      <div class="hero-tracks-preview">
        <a href="#memory-types" class="track-pill">Memory types</a>
        <a href="#where-memory-lives" class="track-pill">Local or shared</a>
        <a href="#2-native-claude-code-memory-stack" class="track-pill track-pill--native">Native Stack</a>
        <a href="#3-cross-session-tools-single-user" class="track-pill track-pill--cross">Cross-Session Tools</a>
        <a href="#4-team-sharing" class="track-pill track-pill--team">Team + Multi-Agent</a>
        <a href="#memory-pipeline" class="track-pill">Is it used?</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <!-- TL;DR -->
    <section class="section section--tldr band">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="tldr-grid">
          <div class="tldr-card tldr-native brand-card">
            <div class="tldr-icon">📄</div>
            <h3>Native stack</h3>
            <p>Zero setup, works today. CLAUDE.md for permanent instructions, Auto Memory for automatic cross-session notes, Auto Dream for background consolidation.</p>
            <span class="tldr-badge">Built-in</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tldr-card tldr-cross brand-card">
            <div class="tldr-icon">🔄</div>
            <h3>Cross-session tools</h3>
            <p>Community-built hooks and MCP servers that persist what Claude does automatically. claude-mem, agentmemory, ICM, Kairn, doobidoo:each with a different recall model.</p>
            <span class="tldr-badge tldr-badge--community">Community</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tldr-card tldr-team brand-card">
            <div class="tldr-icon">👥</div>
            <h3>Team + multi-agent</h3>
            <p>Sharing memory across developers or autonomous agents. The Trinity (zero infra), Mem0 Cloud, Zep/Graphiti. Concurrent-write coordination via leases and signals.</p>
            <span class="tldr-badge tldr-badge--advanced">Advanced</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Memory types -->
    <section class="section section--alt" id="memory-types">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge">Concepts</span>
        <ChapterLabel number="01" label="Memory types" />
        <h2 class="section-title">Memory types: coordinates, not a stack</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Popular diagrams stack five layers (working, episodic, semantic, procedural, "meta-memory") and pair each with a brain region. The research literature classifies memory on independent axes instead, and one artifact has a value on every axis.</p>
        <div class="tool-table-wrap">
          <table class="tool-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Axis</th><th>Values</th><th>Claude Code example</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {memoryAxes.map(a => (
                <tr><td><strong>{a.axis}</strong></td><td>{a.values}</td><td>{a.example}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-notes">
          <p><strong>"Meta-memory" is the wrong word for housekeeping.</strong> In psychology it means monitoring your own remembering. Retention, deduplication and decay are memory management.</p>
          <p><strong>Storage choices are not definitions.</strong> Episodic memory does not have to be append-only, and procedural memory is not only a skill registry: instructions, code and model weights count too. Treat brain-region pairings as an analogy, not anatomy.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#memory-types-coordinates-not-a-stack" class="native-guide-link">Sources and details in the guide &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Where memory lives -->
    <section class="section band" id="where-memory-lives">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge">Scope</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Where memory lives: local, repository, shared</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">"Tied to the project" does not mean "shared with the team". Each row was read in the vendor's documentation on Nov 3, 2026.</p>
        <div class="tool-table-wrap">
          <table class="tool-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Scope</th><th>Shared how</th><th>Lifecycle</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {memoryScopes.map(s => (
                <tr><td><strong>{s.mechanism}</strong></td><td>{s.scope}</td><td>{s.shared}</td><td>{s.lifecycle}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-notes">
          <p><strong>Two designs dominate.</strong> Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI keep learned memory on one machine and share only files people curate (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, skills). Copilot shares learned repository facts with the team and compensates with code citations, write-access gating and expiry. Neither spreads one person's learned memory to a team by accident.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#where-memory-lives-local-repository-shared" class="native-guide-link">Sources per vendor &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Native Stack -->
    <section class="section section--alt" id="2-native-claude-code-memory-stack">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge section-badge--native">Track 1</span>
        <ChapterLabel number="02" label="Native stack" />
        <h2 class="section-title">Native Claude Code memory stack</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Four mechanisms built directly into Claude Code: no extra dependencies, no API costs for the memory layer itself.</p>
        <div class="native-grid">
          {nativeTools.map(tool => (
            <div class="native-card brand-card" id={tool.anchor}>
              <div class="native-card-header">
                <h3 class="native-card-name">{tool.name}</h3>
                <span class={`native-badge native-badge--${tool.badgeColor}`}>{tool.badge}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="native-card-desc">{tool.desc}</p>
              <ul class="native-highlights">
                {tool.highlights.map(h => <li>{h}</li>)}
              </ul>
              <a href={`/guide/memory-systems/#${tool.anchor}`} class="native-guide-link">Full details in guide &rarr;</a>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cross-Session Tools -->
    <section class="section band" id="3-cross-session-tools-single-user">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge section-badge--cross">Track 2</span>
        <ChapterLabel number="03" label="Cross-session" />
        <h2 class="section-title">Cross-session tools</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Community tools that persist memory beyond what the native stack offers. Each uses a different retrieval model: choose based on what you need to remember. Facts for claude-mem and agentmemory were read at their release tags (v15.9.0 and v0.9.31).</p>

        <div class="tool-table-wrap">
          <table class="tool-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Tool</th>
                <th>Approach</th>
                <th>Storage</th>
                <th>Cost</th>
                <th>Recall</th>
                <th>Install</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {crossSessionTools.map(t => (
                <tr class={t.highlight ? 'row-highlight' : ''}>
                  <td>
                    <a href={`/guide/memory-systems/#${t.anchor}`} class="tool-name-link">
                      {t.name}
                      {t.stars && <span class="tool-stars">★ {t.stars}</span>}
                    </a>
                  </td>
                  <td>{t.approach}</td>
                  <td>{t.storage}</td>
                  <td>{t.cost}</td>
                  <td class="recall-cell">{t.recall}</td>
                  <td>{t.install ? <code>{t.install}</code> : 'See the project README'}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="stars-verified">Star counts verified Nov 3, 2026 via the GitHub API.</p>

        <div class="tool-notes">
          <p><strong>Start with:</strong> claude-mem if you want automatic capture that is injected at every session start. agentmemory if you want local-only hybrid retrieval and are ready to opt in to injection (<code>AGENTMEMORY_INJECT_CONTEXT=true</code>); without it, the agent only pulls memory through MCP. memorix if several coding agents on one machine should share one project memory. ICM if you need both episodic memory and a typed relation graph (<code>brew install icm</code>).</p>
          <p><strong>claude-mem, read before installing:</strong> secret redaction is off by default (<code>CLAUDE_MEM_REDACT_ENABLED</code>). From v15.2.0 to v15.8.2, the browser setup uploaded the last 30 days of prompts to cmem.ai without asking; v15.8.3 (Nov 2, 2026) fixed it.</p>
          <p><strong>Known issue (doobidoo):</strong> concurrent access errors under parallel agents: fix with <code>busy_timeout=15000</code> in config.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Team Sharing -->
    <section class="section section--alt" id="4-team-sharing">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge section-badge--team">Track 3</span>
        <ChapterLabel number="04" label="Team sharing" />
        <h2 class="section-title">Team + multi-agent sharing</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Sharing memory across developers or autonomous agents is the hardest problem. Structural reasons, primarily the lack of a standardized conflict-resolution protocol across tools, mean no current solution handles all cases cleanly.</p>

        <div class="team-options">
          {teamOptions.map(opt => (
            <div class="team-card brand-card" id={opt.anchor}>
              <div class="team-card-header">
                <h3>{opt.name}</h3>
                <span class={`team-badge team-badge--${opt.badgeColor}`}>{opt.badge}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="team-card-desc">{opt.desc}</p>
              <div class="team-card-when">
                <span class="when-label">Best when:</span> {opt.when}
              </div>
              <a href={`/guide/memory-systems/#${opt.anchor}`} class="native-guide-link">Full details &rarr;</a>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="multi-agent-note">
          <h3>Multi-agent concurrent writes</h3>
          <p>When multiple agents write to the same memory store, you need coordination. The standard pattern: lease acquisition before write, signal-based invalidation on conflict, bounded retry with exponential backoff. No current tool implements this natively:it requires application-level wrapping.</p>
          <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#5-multi-agent-shared-memory" class="native-guide-link">Multi-agent memory patterns &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Is it used? -->
    <section class="section band" id="memory-pipeline">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="section-badge">Evidence</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Is your memory used? From captured to effective</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Each step can fail without raising an error. Most tools and benchmarks stop at "retrievable": they check that the right item comes back, not that the agent used it.</p>
        <ol class="pipeline-steps">
          {pipelineStages.map((stage, i) => (
            <li class={i >= 4 ? 'pipeline-step pipeline-step--rare' : 'pipeline-step'}>{stage}</li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <div class="multi-agent-note">
          <h3>What a 2026 measurement found</h3>
          <p>MemUse (arXiv 2611.01718, a November 2026 preprint) followed memory delivered in 8,000 SWE-bench Verified episodes from three memory systems. Resolve rates stayed within 0.4 points of a no-memory baseline. Most delivered entries were irrelevant, most relevant entries went unused, and in two systems most deliveries repeated what the agent could already see. One preprint does not settle every workload; it does justify testing your own.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-notes">
          <p><strong>Before adopting or keeping a memory layer:</strong> count items at each stage, plant a canary (write one synthetic observation, open a fresh session or another agent, check it comes back in the right scope and nowhere else), then run your own tasks with memory on and off. Among the tools above, only Graft publishes such an A/B, on 15 questions.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#from-captured-to-effective-the-six-stage-pipeline" class="native-guide-link">Pipeline and 2026 usage benchmarks &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Decision Frameworks -->
    <section class="section band band-ink section-wallpaper" id="8-decision-frameworks">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="section-title">Decision framework: Which track?</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Six rules that cover 90% of real situations. Each maps a concrete problem to the right solution layer.</p>

        <div class="decision-rules">
          {decisionRules.map(rule => (
            <div class={`decision-rule decision-rule--${rule.track}`}>
              <div class="rule-condition">
                <span class="rule-if">If:</span>
                <span>{rule.condition}</span>
              </div>
              <div class="rule-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
              <div class="rule-answer">{rule.answer}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="decision-cta brand-card">
          <p>The full guide includes a Mermaid decision flowchart covering edge cases: offline constraints, security requirements, data sensitivity, and multi-agent coordination.</p>
          <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#81-decision-flowchart" class="btn btn-primary">Full decision flowchart &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Risks & Architecture -->
    <section class="section section--alt">
      <div class="container">
        <ChapterLabel number="05" label="Risks" />
        <h2 class="section-title">Key risks</h2>
        <p class="section-subtitle">Memory systems introduce attack surfaces and failure modes that don't exist with stateless contexts.</p>
        <div class="risks-grid">
          <div class="risk-card risk-card--high brand-card">
            <div class="risk-header">
              <span class="risk-level">High</span>
              <h3>Memory poisoning</h3>
            </div>
            <p>An adversarial input causes the agent to store false beliefs or instructions in its memory store. On later sessions, they influence behavior. 2026 preprints (MemGhost, MemHarmBench) show agents completing legitimate tasks normally while following the planted intent. Write validation is still not a default anywhere.</p>
            <a href="/guide/memory-systems/#71-memory-poisoning-via-prompt-injection" class="native-guide-link">Details &rarr;</a>
          </div>
          <div class="risk-card risk-card--medium brand-card">
            <div class="risk-header">
              <span class="risk-level">Medium</span>
              <h3>Context inflation</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Memory retrieval injects tokens at session start. If retrieval is poorly tuned, you can end up with more context tokens from memory than from the actual task, degrading adherence.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="risk-card risk-card--medium brand-card">
            <div class="risk-header">
              <span class="risk-level">Medium</span>
              <h3>Stale beliefs</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Memories stored months ago may reflect outdated codebase state, team conventions, or user preferences. Decay models (Kairn, ICM) partially address this; flat storage (CLAUDE.md, doobidoo) does not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="risk-card risk-card--low brand-card">
            <div class="risk-header">
              <span class="risk-level">Low</span>
              <h3>Storage fragmentation</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Each tool uses its own storage format. Migrating from claude-mem to ICM, or from CLAUDE.md to Mem0, has no standard export path. Lock-in is mild (stores are local files) but migration is manual.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
